//! The random bytes the transport draws (A-594): a KEXINIT's cookie (RFC 4253 §7.1, "a random value generated by the
//! sender"), each packet's padding (§6, "The padding SHOULD consist of random bytes") and each exchange's ephemeral
//! X25519 key (RFC 7748 §6.1, "32 random bytes").
//!
//! The transport draws them through [`Entropy`], a seam with one real implementation, [`SystemEntropy`]: the operating
//! system's generator through AWS-LC (A-66). The seam keeps the core sans-io and lets the tests run whole exchanges from
//! fixed bytes, so a transcript with known keys is reproducible and a golden vector's ephemeral key can be the
//! document's own. A draw that fails ends the connection with a typed refusal; nothing is ever drawn from a weaker source.
//!
//! **One draw does not pass through it** (A-634): the 32 random bytes of the hybrid's ML-KEM encapsulation (FIPS 203
//! §7.2, `m`). aws-lc-rs encapsulates only from AWS-LC's own generator, which is the generator [`SystemEntropy`] reads:
//! mlkem-native's `randombytes` is AWS-LC's `RAND_bytes` (`mlkem_native_config.h` in the vendored aws-lc-sys 0.45.0),
//! and a failed draw there aborts inside AWS-LC, as `RAND_bytes` does wherever it fails. AWS-LC's encapsulation from
//! given bytes, `EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_deterministic`, is in its experimental API and aws-lc-rs does not wrap it, so the
//! transport does not reach it. In production the source is the same; in the tests a hybrid exchange's transcript is
//! not reproducible from the seeds, while a `curve25519-sha256` exchange's still is.

use aws_lc_rs::rand::{SecureRandom, SystemRandom};

/// A draw of random bytes that failed.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct EntropyFailed;

/// A source of random bytes.
pub trait Entropy {
  /// Fills `out` with random bytes, or says the source failed.
  fn fill(&mut self, out: &mut [u8]) -> Result<(), EntropyFailed>;
}

/// The operating system's generator, through AWS-LC.
#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub struct SystemEntropy {
  /// AWS-LC's handle on the generator.
  generator: SystemRandom,
}

impl SystemEntropy {
  /// The system generator.
  pub fn new() -> Self {
    Self {
      generator: SystemRandom::new(),
    }
  }
}

impl Entropy for SystemEntropy {
  fn fill(&mut self, out: &mut [u8]) -> Result<(), EntropyFailed> {
    self.generator.fill(out).map_err(|_| EntropyFailed)
  }
}
